  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Letters, syllables and the tsheg",
      "body": "<p>Tibetan is written from left to right with letters hanging from a headline. Words are not separated by spaces; instead every syllable ends with a small dot called the <i>tsheg</i> (<span lang=\"bo\">་</span>), and a vertical stroke, the <i>shad</i> (<span lang=\"bo\">།</span>), ends a phrase or sentence.</p>\n<p>A syllable is built around one root letter. Other letters can sit in front of it, on top of it, below it or after it: <span lang=\"bo\">བསྒྲུབས</span> (bsgrubs, accomplished) has five letters and a vowel sign around the root <span lang=\"bo\">ག</span> but is said in one beat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Spelling and sound",
      "body": "<p>Tibetan spelling was fixed more than a thousand years ago, and the spoken language has changed a lot since. Many letters that were once said are now silent: <span lang=\"bo\">བསྒྲུབས</span> is said drub. Letters before the root and on top of it mostly change only the tone, and some letters after the root change the vowel: <span lang=\"bo\">གོས</span> (gos) is said gö.</p>\n<p>That is why there are two kinds of romanization. Wylie, used in this app, spells out every letter so the Tibetan can be rebuilt from it. Phonetic systems write what is said instead, so the same word might be written bsgrubs in Wylie and drub in phonetics.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "High and low tones",
      "body": "<p>Lhasa Tibetan has high and low tones, and the alphabet shows them. In the first five rows of four, the first two letters have a high tone and the last two a low tone. The third letter of each of those rows, like <span lang=\"bo\">ག</span> (ga), was once a g; in Lhasa it is now said like kh with a low tone.</p>\n<p>A letter written in front of or on top of a low root can make it high again: <span lang=\"bo\">ན</span> is low, but <span lang=\"bo\">སྣ</span> (sna, nose) is high.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
